WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
When to route to a person.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:06.296
Human review is not a failure of automation; it’s a component you design.

3
00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:19.163
Decide the routing policy in advance and write it down: Always review items that failed a deterministic check, items where the checker said no or unclear, and items where multiple samples disagreed.

4
00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:20.573
Review by stakes.

5
00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:22.603
Some fields cost more when wrong.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:30.307
A due date promised to a client or an owner assigned to a sensitive task may deserve review even when every check passes.

7
00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:31.931
Audit what you accept.

8
00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:37.181
Randomly sample a few auto-accepted items each week and have a person check them.

9
00:00:37.461 --> 00:00:47.061
That sample is your estimate of the error rate in the part of the pipeline nobody else looks at, and it’s how you notice drift when transcripts, prompts, or models change.

10
00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:55.853
Track two numbers over time: the share of items sent to review (your cost) and the error rate in the audited auto-accepted items (your risk).

11
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:02.427
Tightening checks moves cost up and risk down; your job is to pick the trade-off on purpose, with evidence.

12
00:01:02.707 --> 00:01:07.549
In the studio project, the optional interactive review step is exactly this queue.

13
00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:13.440
On model selection: all of these checks are model-agnostic, which is what makes them valuable.

14
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:20.632
They let you try a cheaper extractor and measure, rather than guess, what it costs you in review volume and audited errors.
